## TL;DR: 5 Mistakes Radiant Cut Engagement Ring Buyers Always Make

**The 5 mistakes — read these before anything else:**
1. **Not checking the L/W ratio** — "radiant cut" is two different rings. Elongated (1.20–1.40) looks nothing like square (0.95–1.05). Buyers order one, receive the other, and have to return it.
2. **Paying for VS1 clarity** — VS2 is the clarity floor for radiant cut. The 70 facets hide inclusions that a step-cut would expose. VS1 is wasted money on this shape.
3. **Budgeting stone and setting separately** — the Total Ring Number is one figure: stone + setting combined. Split it wrong (too much on the setting) and you end up with a cheap stone in an expensive frame.
4. **Ruling out halos** — buyers who read the emerald cut's "no halo" rule assume it applies here. It doesn't. The radiant's clipped corners work with a round halo. The [Twisted Halo RG at $2,250](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/twisted-halo-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-1-3-ct-tw-item-194950?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a genuine option.
5. **Comparing radiant to round brilliant pricing** — radiant cuts are underpriced relative to ovals and rounds at the same carat weight and quality. Most buyers don't know this. It means you get more stone for less money.

