## What Is a Radiant Cut Engagement Ring?

A radiant cut engagement ring pairs the radiant cut diamond — the only shape that combines step-cut elegance with brilliant-cut fire — with a setting designed to let that dual character show.

I'm **Farzana Hasan**, a GIA Expert. The radiant cut is the one shape where you have complete setting freedom. I have never seen a setting that looks actively wrong on a radiant cut. The 70-facet structure fires in so many directions that it overpowers any setting weakness. But that freedom is also the trap. Because buyers can choose anything, they often choose the wrong thing — not the wrong setting, but the wrong *shape* of stone.

Full credentials and methodology at **[Diamond Critics](https://diamondcritics.com/about-farzana/)**.

The radiant cut was created in 1977 by Henry Grossbard. He wanted the large face-up table of a step-cut with the sparkle of a brilliant. The 70 facets on the crown and pavilion were the solution. No other shape has that combination. An emerald cut has the large table but flat flash. A round brilliant has the sparkle but a smaller visible area. The radiant cut has both.

For an engagement ring, this matters because the radiant cut looks good at every carat size. At 1ct, it fires brilliantly. At 2ct, the elongated version creates a visual effect similar to an oval or emerald — elegant and finger-lengthening. At 3ct, it becomes a serious statement piece that works in every light condition.

