**Rank #1 — Riviera Pavé WG ($1,500, 479 reviews).** The Riviera Pavé is the most versatile radiant cut setting available. The thin pavé band adds shoulder sparkle without adding any visual bulk around the center stone. It works equally well with elongated and square radiant cuts. It is the most popular pairing in current certified market data.

**Rank #2 — Classic Four-Prong Platinum ($1,180, 1,815 reviews).** The most-reviewed setting in the database for a reason. Four prongs hold the corners of the radiant cut cleanly. Platinum is the most durable setting metal — it doesn't wear thin over time the way white gold does. This is the right choice for buyers who want a classic, low-maintenance ring that lasts decades.

**Rank #3 — Twisted Halo Rose Gold ($2,250, 236 reviews).** The halo works on a radiant cut in a way it doesn't on an emerald cut. The radiant cut's corners are clipped and rounded, so a round halo doesn't fight the stone outline. The twisted halo in rose gold is a particularly strong pairing with a square radiant — it adds warmth and softens the stone's edges.

[![Radiant cut engagement ring rose gold pavé setting on model hand](/images/radiant-cut-engagement-ring-rose-gold-pavé.avif)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?ShapeID=Radiant&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Rank #4 — Petite Hidden Halo Yellow Gold ($1,255, 105 reviews).** Yellow gold with a radiant cut is a classic pairing. The stone's warm fire looks even richer against yellow gold. The hidden halo adds melee diamonds under the center stone — visible from the side, invisible from above. Affordable and distinctive.

**Rank #5 — Criss Cross Solitaire Rose Gold ($1,650, 20 reviews).** The criss-cross band crosses beneath the stone, creating a modern geometric look that complements an elongated radiant particularly well. Fewer reviews means fewer buyers have tested it, so rank accordingly. But the visual result is strong.

