## Metal Options for Radiant Cut Settings

[![Radiant cut engagement ring rose gold solitaire on white background](/images/radiant-cut-engagement-ring-rose-gold-solitaire.avif)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?ShapeID=Radiant&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

The radiant cut works in every metal. This is one of its advantages over step-cut shapes like the emerald cut, which look best in white metals. The radiant cut's 70 facets fire warmly in yellow gold and softly in rose gold — both look intentional, not accidental.

**White gold and platinum.** White metals are the most popular choice. They don't add a colour cast to the stone, which matters if you're buying G colour or above. Platinum is more durable and denser — it costs more but lasts longer. White gold requires rhodium plating every few years. For daily-wear rings, platinum is worth the premium.

**Yellow gold.** An H or I colour radiant cut in yellow gold is a smart choice. Yellow gold naturally compensates for slight body warmth in the stone, which means you can buy a slightly lower colour grade and save money without any visible compromise. The [Petite Hidden Halo Solitaire YG at $1,255](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/petite-hidden-halo-solitaire-plus-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-item-192506?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a great entry-level yellow gold option with 105 reviews.

**Rose gold.** Rose gold works particularly well with a square radiant — the soft pink metal and the compact stone shape create a romantic, feminine ring. The [Twisted Halo in rose gold at $2,250](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/twisted-halo-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-1-3-ct-tw-item-194950?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the strongest rose gold setting for a radiant cut.

